Dialogue: 3,0:00:01.25,0:00:07.00,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}EMERGENCY HOSPITAL DURING THE INFLUENZA EPIDEMIC
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.61,0:00:29.15,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}And in the United States, CDC says, it was first
Dialogue: 0,0:00:29.17,0:00:31.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}identified in military personnel.
Dialogue: 2,0:00:25.86,0:00:31.61,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:00:25.86,0:00:31.61,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:00:25.86,0:00:31.61,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"INFANTRY TRAINING" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1179
Dialogue: 3,0:00:25.86,0:00:31.61,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:00:32.07,0:00:35.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}This is how the deadliest flu in modern history went
Dialogue: 0,0:00:35.20,0:00:37.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}to war, and what medicine learned from it.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:45.79,0:00:48.84,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}When America entered the First World War, more than
Dialogue: 0,0:00:48.86,0:00:50.89,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}4 million recruits were mobilized.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.91,0:00:53.79,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}About thirty training camps were thrown up fast.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:53.81,0:00:56.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Some held up to 50,000 men, in crowded barracks.
Dialogue: 2,0:00:46.04,0:00:51.79,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:00:46.04,0:00:51.79,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:00:46.04,0:00:51.79,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"INFANTRY TRAINING" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1179
Dialogue: 3,0:00:46.04,0:00:51.79,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:01:23.63,0:01:26.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In the weeks that followed, thousands of soldiers
Dialogue: 0,0:01:26.84,0:01:29.43,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}there came down with influenza. 38 died.
Dialogue: 2,0:01:23.88,0:01:29.63,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1160 0 l 1160 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:01:23.88,0:01:29.63,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:01:23.88,0:01:29.63,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}CAMP FUNSTON, KANSAS · 1918 · NATIONAL MUSEUM OF HEALTH AND MEDICINE
Dialogue: 3,0:01:23.88,0:01:29.63,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}EMERGENCY HOSPITAL DURING THE INFLUENZA EPIDEMIC
Dialogue: 0,0:01:47.76,0:01:50.99,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}CDC says the mass movement of troops helped spread
Dialogue: 0,0:01:51.01,0:01:52.61,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}the flu around the world.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:52.63,0:01:55.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Within weeks, camps in Georgia reported cases.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:55.62,0:01:58.91,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}By the end of April, 24 of the Army's 36 main camps
Dialogue: 0,0:01:58.93,0:02:00.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}had reported outbreaks.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:00.42,0:02:03.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}And influenza reached Brest, in France, a major port
Dialogue: 0,0:02:03.80,0:02:05.08,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}for American troops.
Dialogue: 2,0:01:48.01,0:01:53.76,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:01:48.01,0:01:53.76,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:01:48.01,0:01:53.76,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"INFANTRY TRAINING" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1179
Dialogue: 3,0:01:48.01,0:01:53.76,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.17,0:02:19.97,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}That first wave was widespread.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:19.99,0:02:21.80,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}But the worst was still coming.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.81,0:03:47.71,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}With so many doctors and nurses serving in the
Dialogue: 0,0:03:47.73,0:03:50.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}military, CDC says, there were shortages of medical
Dialogue: 0,0:03:50.96,0:03:52.78,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}personnel across the country.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:52.80,0:03:55.82,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In Missouri, roughly half of the state's doctors
Dialogue: 0,0:03:55.84,0:03:57.66,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}were serving in the military.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:57.68,0:04:00.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In October, the Army stopped taking in new draftees
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.92,0:04:02.48,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}until the epidemic ended.
Dialogue: 2,0:03:45.06,0:03:50.81,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:03:45.06,0:03:50.81,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:03:45.06,0:03:50.81,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"INFANTRY TRAINING" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1179
Dialogue: 3,0:03:45.06,0:03:50.81,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.95,0:04:04.64,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}But the war still needed men.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:04.66,0:04:07.69,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}The Army's Surgeon General recommended halting troop
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.71,0:04:08.86,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}shipments to France.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:08.88,0:04:10.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}President Wilson declined.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.41,0:04:12.68,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}General Pershing wanted reinforcements.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.70,0:04:13.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}And they sailed.
Dialogue: 2,0:04:03.20,0:04:08.95,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:03.20,0:04:08.95,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:03.20,0:04:08.95,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"INFANTRY TRAINING" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1179
Dialogue: 3,0:04:03.20,0:04:08.95,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.09,0:04:16.98,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}These Army films, from 1919, show what those
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.00,0:04:18.50,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}troopships looked like.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:18.52,0:04:21.94,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Soldiers packed shoulder to shoulder, for a crossing
Dialogue: 0,0:04:21.96,0:04:23.07,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}of 10 to 14 days.
Dialogue: 2,0:04:14.34,0:04:20.09,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1338 0 l 1338 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:14.34,0:04:20.09,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:14.34,0:04:20.09,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1454
Dialogue: 3,0:04:14.34,0:04:20.09,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1919 · TROOPS BOARD TRANSPORTS, ST. NAZAIRE
Dialogue: 0,0:04:23.54,0:04:26.60,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Healthy soldiers often fell sick on the way.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:26.62,0:04:30.18,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}One sailor counted 15 burials at sea, from a single
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.20,0:04:31.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}ship, in one day.
Dialogue: 2,0:04:23.79,0:04:29.54,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1338 0 l 1338 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:23.79,0:04:29.54,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:23.79,0:04:29.54,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1454
Dialogue: 3,0:04:23.79,0:04:29.54,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1919 · TROOPS BOARD TRANSPORTS, ST. NAZAIRE
Dialogue: 0,0:04:48.08,0:04:51.37,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}In October, during the Meuse-Argonne offensive,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:51.39,0:04:53.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}1,451 Americans died of the flu.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:53.64,0:04:57.21,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}The Third Infantry Division evacuated more soldiers
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.23,0:05:01.01,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}from the front with influenza than with combat wounds.
Dialogue: 2,0:04:48.33,0:04:54.08,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:48.33,0:04:54.08,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:04:48.33,0:04:54.08,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"WELFARE ACTIVITIES: RED CROSS" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1540
Dialogue: 3,0:04:48.33,0:04:54.08,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:05:11.55,0:05:13.63,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Much of the care fell to nurses.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:13.65,0:05:16.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}By the Armistice, the Army Nurse Corps had reached
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.92,0:05:19.39,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}an estimated peak of more than 21,000.
Dialogue: 2,0:05:11.80,0:05:17.55,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:05:11.80,0:05:17.55,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:05:11.80,0:05:17.55,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}"WELFARE ACTIVITIES: RED CROSS" · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 111-H-1540
Dialogue: 3,0:05:11.80,0:05:17.55,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}ARCHIVAL FILM · 1918–1919 · U.S. ARMY SIGNAL CORPS
Dialogue: 0,0:05:31.52,0:05:34.62,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}Back home, Red Cross workers made masks for soldiers
Dialogue: 0,0:05:34.64,0:05:35.09,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}in camp.
Dialogue: 2,0:05:31.77,0:05:35.51,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1124 0 l 1124 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:05:31.77,0:05:35.51,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H00A3B32B\1a&H00&\p1}m 0 0 l 10 0 l 10 132 l 0 132{\p0}
Dialogue: 3,0:05:31.77,0:05:35.51,Kick,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,846,126,846,0,350)\fad(250,300)}BOSTON, MASS. · 1918 · NATIONAL ARCHIVES 165-WW-269B
Dialogue: 3,0:05:31.77,0:05:35.51,Head,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(106,878,126,878,0,350)\fad(250,300)}RED CROSS WORKERS MAKE MASKS FOR SOLDIERS IN CAMP
Dialogue: 0,0:05:54.63,0:05:57.90,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}And the camps were full of young men, an age group
Dialogue: 0,0:05:57.92,0:06:00.40,Sub,,0,0,0,,{\fad(80,80)}usually less affected by seasonal flu.
Dialogue: 2,0:05:54.88,0:06:00.63,Box,,0,0,0,,{\an7\move(76,828,96,828,0,350)\fad(250,300)\bord0\shad0\1c&H0014120A\1a&H30&\p1}m 0 0 l 1146 0 l 1146 132 l 0 132{\p0}
